From 8785bc64b512ff2256e36272a338b1072833102f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:44:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] primitives: inline small_vector storage for CTransaction
 inputs and outputs

Each CTransaction allocated two heap blocks for its vin and vout
std::vectors. Store them in boost::container::small_vector with room
for one input and two outputs inline, covering the common payment
shape, so such transactions need no separate vin/vout allocations and
their elements sit next to the transaction header. Larger transactions
spill to the heap exactly like std::vector.

prevector cannot be used here: it requires trivially copyable elements
and CTxIn/CTxOut own CScripts. CMutableTransaction keeps std::vector.

- SerializeTransaction writes inputs/outputs through
  SerializeTxElements, producing the same bytes as std::vector
  serialization for any container.
- CTransaction is built from CMutableTransaction by element-wise copy
  or move; moving still steals each element's script storage.
- The deserializing constructor reads straight into the inline
  containers instead of through a CMutableTransaction, whose
  std::vectors would otherwise allocate on every P2P tx and block
  transaction. UnserializeTransaction calls the same
  VectorFormatter<DefaultFormatter> that std::vector deserialization
  uses, so wire format and the batched-allocation DoS limit are
  unchanged for both types.
- core_memusage counts small_vector heap usage only once it spills.
- CheckMinerFund takes Span<const CTxOut>.
- boost/container/small_vector.hpp is added to the Boost lint
  whitelist; it is header-only.

sizeof(CTransaction) grows from 192 to 352 bytes on LP64. The inline
space is wasted for transactions that spill (more than one input or
more than two outputs).

Test: serialization is byte-identical to CMutableTransaction across
0-3 inputs and 0-4 outputs (inline and spilled), round-trips, and
memory accounting reports no heap use while inline.
